From 32e848083ed2751a846ff2755750d721b29c302c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ryoma-89 <ryoma-89@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 13:07:46 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] velox: publish native fence completions to a guest-readable
 table (patch 0021)

VELOX_FENCE_SHARED=1 (guest debug.velox.fence_shared=1). Each ASG context
gets a 64-slot table in the unused tail of its from_host_large_xfer ring page
(offset 2560). When a fence the guest submitted on that context reaches
VK_SUCCESS natively, the host writes "boxed handle + reset epoch" into the
table (seqlock per slot). The epoch counts vkResetFences processed for the
fence, so a completion from before a reset never matches the guest's
current epoch. The guest answers vkGetFenceStatus/vkWaitForFences locally
only on an exact handle+epoch match and otherwise uses the RPC.

Completions come from the host's own observations: a per-device watcher
thread that waits (2 ms slices) on fences that stayed pending for
VELOX_FENCE_WATCH_DELAY_US (100) without anyone observing them, and the
RPC paths (vkGetFenceStatus / vkWaitForFences returning VK_SUCCESS). The
watcher is never woken by a submission (it polls every 250 us while it has
work), never touches a fence that the decoder observed first, and is
cancelled (waiting for any native call on the fence to return) before a
fence is reset or destroyed; it is stopped before a device's objects are
destroyed and stops publishing after VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST. The host never
reads the table; its decisions use FenceInfo, so a guest that scribbles
over the page only confuses its own queries. The table is invalidated
before the ASG context frees the ring storage and after a snapshot load.

Also: thread names and optional priority for the watcher, and
VELOX_<ROLE>_PRIORITY (above_normal / highest) for the Velox host threads.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
